# Line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: this repo is checked out on the Windows/NTFS side of
# windows-pc as well as on macOS/Linux. With no attributes and core.autocrlf
# unset both globally and system-wide there, a Windows-side write rewrote whole
# files to CRLF. Git then reported those files as "modified" while every one of
# them was byte-identical to HEAD after stripping CR. That phantom dirt is
# enough to make ml fleet-catchup / safe_pull refuse the host with "dirty
# working tree (unstaged changes) - refusing reset" and pin it silently; it did
# exactly that to lawrieverse-infra (28 files, 52 commits behind).
#
# core.autocrlf is per-clone and does not travel between clones or hosts.
# A committed .gitattributes does. That is why this is a tracked file and not a
# config knob.
#
# The correct rule is repo-specific, not copy-pasteable: use "-text" only where
# the committed blob is genuinely CRLF (pinning bytes on both sides), and "text"
# where the committed blob is LF. Pinning -text over an LF blob would freeze the
# worktree CRLF against it and keep the phantom modification alive.
#
# gitattributes is "last match wins": the broad rule MUST come first and the
# specific overrides below intentionally win over it.

* text=auto

# Interpreter scripts: ALWAYS LF in the working tree, on every platform. A CRLF
# in a shebang ("#!/usr/bin/env sh\r") makes the kernel look for an interpreter
# literally named "sh\r" and the script fails to launch.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.zsh  text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts: every blob of these types in this repo's history is
# committed with LF, so they get "text" (normalise on checkin), NOT "-text".
*.ps1 text
*.vbs text
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touched.
*.exe binary
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz  binary
